# The v4->v6 adb forwarder as an ANDROID INIT SERVICE (bd remora-yn4).
#
# Bind-mounted to /vendor/etc/init/remora-fwd.rc by buildDockerRunArgv, so it needs no image
# change and init parses it on every boot alongside the vendor HAL .rc files.
#
# Why init rather than the shell supervisor in remora-netfix.sh: init starts this on EVERY
# container start, including ones Remora knows nothing about. A container restarted outside a
# deploy — a bare `docker start`, a restart policy, whatever restarted this instance
# came back with Android healthy and NO forwarder, so adb and the mirror were dead
# until the next deploy re-ran netfix. Nothing in Remora could have noticed: it was not running.
#
# init also supervises for free. A non-oneshot service is restarted when it exits, which is the
# same job the netfix shell loop does but done by PID 1, so it cannot itself be killed by whatever
# has been killing fwd — and init LOGS each death to logcat ("Service 'remora_fwd' ... exited"),
# which is a second, independent record beside the supervisor's own log.
#
# /remora/fwd exists only because Remora mounted it. A container without that bind logs a failure
# to start and carries on booting; nothing else depends on this service.
# STARTED AFTER BOOT, not with its class. `class main` runs this before the container's network
# exists — eth0 is renamed into the namespace roughly a minute after init begins — and fwd exits
# status 1 when it cannot bind, so init restarted it every 5s until the network arrived: measured
# at 15 failed starts per boot, and enough for init to log "exited 4 times in 4 minutes". It
# converged, but a service that flaps fifteen times on every boot is noise that would bury the
# ONE exit worth reading (the spontaneous death this bead is still chasing).
#
# `disabled` only suppresses the automatic start with the class; once started, init still restarts
# it whenever it exits, so the supervision this is here for is unaffected.
# Run through a shell purely to KEEP fwd's OUTPUT. init discards a service's stdout/stderr, so an
# init-started fwd said nothing at all — its own "[fwd] bind: ..." line, the one thing that names
# why a start failed, went nowhere, and repeated "exited with status 1" gave no reason. `exec`
# replaces the shell with fwd, so init still tracks and restarts fwd's own pid; the shell is a
# redirection, not a supervisor. Same log the fallback supervisor writes, so there is one place to
# read whichever mechanism is running (bd remora-yn4).
# The loop WAITS for /data rather than failing on it: the start trigger fires on the first
# sys.boot_completed, which this image reaches before /data/local/tmp is writable, so the
# redirection failed and the shell exited before fwd ever ran.
#
# KNOWN RESIDUE, measured and left deliberately: the service still logs ~12 failed starts during
# boot before it sticks (down from ~24 without the wait). They produce NO output in this log, so
# they die before fwd runs — the shell wrapper itself is failing that early — and every one is
# init's own 5s retry, which is why "exited 4 times in 4 minutes" appears. It ALWAYS converges:
# init.svc.remora_fwd reaches "running", 5556 listens, and one fwd survives. Three rounds of
# narrowing (late_start + disabled, a bind-retry in fwd.c, this wait loop) each reduced it without
# removing it, and the remaining cause is not understood. It is noise on a mechanism that works,
# not a failure — but it is noise in exactly the log someone will read to find the spontaneous
# death this whole bead is chasing, so anyone reading here should discard boot-window entries and
# look only at exits AFTER the service has settled.
service remora_fwd /system/bin/sh -c "until mkdir -p /data/local/tmp 2>/dev/null && : >>/data/local/tmp/remora-fwd.log 2>/dev/null; do sleep 1; done; exec /remora/fwd 5556 5555 >>/data/local/tmp/remora-fwd.log 2>&1"
    class late_start
    user root
    group root
    disabled

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    start remora_fwd
